# security-without-authorized-target.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M129): a security assertion with no
# declaration behind it — demonstrates `tflw check`'s TF060 (checker, tflw M128b / D291, the pentest
# arc's Tier 1).
#
# `expect response has no … security violations` is the first construct in this language that scans
# a host rather than asserting about a response it was already given. D21's safety model says a scan
# needs an author affirming in writing that they are permitted to point it there — `authorized
# target "<origin>" reason "<why>"` in `tflw.config` — and this code is that requirement being
# load-bearing from the milestone that introduced the grammar rather than from the one that first
# sends a probe. **A safety control nothing exercises is not a safety control.**
#
# **Matching is by origin — scheme + host + port — not by the pattern rules `allow hosts` uses.** A
# declaration for `https://x.example.com` does not authorize `https://x.example.com:8443`: that is a
# different listener and may belong to a different team. **Loopback is not exempt**, deliberately,
# because exempting it would exempt exactly the target this arc is tested against and ship the
# requirement untested.
#
# **This project's own `tflw.config` is the second operand and declares nothing** — `env local`'s
# `api "http://localhost:4001/v1"` has no `authorized target` beside it, which is the correct state
# for a suite that does no scanning. The declared side lives in `tflw-acceptance/security/`, whose
# own config carries the declaration and the reason; that corpus is where the *passing* form is
# dogfooded. So the two halves of D291 are demonstrated in two places on purpose: refusal here,
# permission there.
#
# **Narrow in the same three ways `TF036` is:** only the env's default `api` base, only a fully
# literal one, and skipped entirely when no config was resolved.
#
# **Not covered:** whether the declared reason is *true*. Nothing can check that, which is why the
# field is prose and why it is embedded in the report next to the findings it permitted.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/security-without-authorized-target.tflw

test "a scan against an env that authorizes nothing"
  api GET /health
  expect response has no security violations

test "a severity floor does not change the requirement"
  api GET /health
  # The floor narrows which rules run; it has nothing to do with permission to run them at all.
  check response has no critical security violations

test "the neighbours the rule must not touch"
  api GET /health
  # Ordinary assertions about a response already in hand — no host is being scanned.
  expect status equals 200
  expect header "content-type" contains "json"
